<h2 id=3D"4">imguiml 1.93.0</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-4">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-imguiml-1-93-0/18672/1"=
>https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-imguiml-1-93-0/18672/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-orgf17ad89" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"orgf17ad89">Fr=C3=A9d=C3=A9ric Bour announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-orgf17ad89">
<p>
I'm happy to announce a new release of imguiml, the OCaml bindings to Dear =
ImGui.
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li>Project page: <a href=3D"https://github.com/let-def/imguiml">https://gi=
thub.com/let-def/imguiml</a></li>
<li>Documentation: <a href=3D"https://let-def.github.io/imguiml">https://le=
t-def.github.io/imguiml</a></li>
</ul>

<p>
I am taking over maintenance of the project from its original author, Emili=
en Lemaire.
</p>

<p>
What's new:
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li>Updated to <b>Dear ImGui 1.93.0</b>, with docking support.</li>
<li>The bindings now work on <b>arm64</b> (and probably s390).</li>
<li>Fonts are now rendered with the system <b>FreeType</b> instead of <code=
>stb_truetype</code>, providing higher-quality rendering and better compati=
bility with modern font formats.</li>
<li>ImGui is now built with support for 32-bit characters, so <b>Emojis</b>=
 are supported.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Disclaimer: "Claude" was used in this port, notably to rebase onto the late=
st version of ImGui and to fix the build on arm64.
</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-5" class=3D"outline-2">
<h2 id=3D"5">Introcaml 5.5.2</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-5">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/introcaml-5-5-2/18673/1">ht=
tps://discuss.ocaml.org/t/introcaml-5-5-2/18673/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-orgb09cd98" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"orgb09cd98">Fr=C3=A9d=C3=A9ric Bour announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-orgb09cd98">
<p>
We're happy to announce a new alpha release of Introcaml, an extension of O=
Caml that brings polymorphic printing. It allows printing and inspecting va=
lues of any type, without writing printer functions. See the <a href=3D"htt=
ps://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-introcaml-alpha-polymorphic-printing-and-intro=
spection-for-ocaml/18407">first announcement</a> for an introduction.
</p>

<p>
Introcaml is developed at <a href=3D"https://shogan.ai">Shogan.ai</a>.
</p>

<p>
This release ports Introcaml to OCaml 5.5.2. To try it:
</p>

<pre class=3D"example" id=3D"org0a2aa74">
opam switch create 5.5.2+introcaml
</pre>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-org91fa6d6" class=3D"outline-4">
<h4 id=3D"org91fa6d6">What's new</h4>
<div class=3D"outline-text-4" id=3D"text-org91fa6d6">
</div>
<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><a id=3D"orgb8884d2"></a>JSON printer<br />
<div class=3D"outline-text-5" id=3D"text-orgb8884d2">
<p>
A generic JSON printer is available in <code>Introspect.Json.*</code>, inte=
nded for debugging (and piping through <a href=3D"https://jqlang.org/">jq</=
a> :sweat_smile:):
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-ocaml"><code># <span style=3D"color: #557400; font-we=
ight: bold;">Introspect.Json.</span>to_channel stdout [
    <span style=3D"color: #242521;">`Point</span> {x =3D 5; y =3D 0};
    <span style=3D"color: #242521;">`String</span> <span style=3D"color: #c=
a3400;">"foo"</span>;
    <span style=3D"color: #242521;">`List</span> [<span style=3D"color: #55=
7400; font-weight: bold;">Result.</span><span style=3D"color: #242521;">Ok<=
/span> <span style=3D"color: #00824f;">true</span>; <span style=3D"color: #=
557400; font-weight: bold;">Result.</span><span style=3D"color: #242521;">E=
rror</span> <span style=3D"color: #00824f;">false</span>]
  ]<span style=3D"color: #9f0d0f;">;;</span>
[{<span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"Point"</span>:{<span style=3D"color: #ca=
3400;">"x"</span>:5,<span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"y"</span>:0}},{<span s=
tyle=3D"color: #ca3400;">"String"</span>:<span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"f=
oo"</span>},{<span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"List"</span>:[{<span style=3D=
"color: #ca3400;">"Ok"</span>:<span style=3D"color: #00824f;">true</span>},=
{<span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"Error"</span>:<span style=3D"color: #0082=
4f;">false</span>}]}]
=2D : unit =3D ()
#=20
</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
<li><a id=3D"org56dd337"></a>Debugging flags<br />
<div class=3D"outline-text-5" id=3D"text-org56dd337">
<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><code>-dblock-desc</code> shows descriptors in <code>-dlambda</code> an=
d similar output.</li>
<li><code>-verbose</code> reports descriptor collisions at link time.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-org1c10d0a" class=3D"outline-4">
<h4 id=3D"org1c10d0a">Compatibility</h4>
<div class=3D"outline-text-4" id=3D"text-org1c10d0a">
<p>
Object files and executables (exec, cmo, cma, cmx, cmxa, cmxs) now use Intr=
ocaml-specific magic numbers (<code>Caml1999?901</code>). cmi, cmt and ast =
files keep the upstream format.
</p>
</div>
<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><a id=3D"orge6133d9"></a>js_of_ocaml support<br />
<div class=3D"outline-text-5" id=3D"text-orge6133d9">
<p>
Although this was done in the js_of_ocaml repository, I think it is relevan=
t to point the work we did @hhugo to bring full compatibility with Introcam=
l. js_of_ocaml can now be installed in Introcaml switches and javascript bu=
ilds can now be instrumented. This brings polymorphic printing to OCaml pro=
grams compiled with js_of_ocaml, at no observable runtime cost (in our expe=
riments).
</p>

<p>
There is also opt-in integration with the browser's JavaScript console (<co=
de>console.log</code>) and debugger, though this appears to be Chrome-only =
as far as I can tell. See <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-js-of-=
ocaml-wasm-of-ocaml-6-5/18632/3">how to enable it</a>.
</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-orgc731228" class=3D"outline-4">
<h4 id=3D"orgc731228">Bug fixes</h4>
<div class=3D"outline-text-4" id=3D"text-orgc731228">
<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><b>Descriptor collisions:</b> descriptors are hashed better, reducing c=
ollisions. When they still collide, values are printed raw instead of with =
the wrong names.</li>
<li><b>Descriptor representation:</b> the representation is simpler and mor=
e portable.</li>
<li><b>Missing descriptors:</b> I fixed a few cases where descriptors were =
omitted or lost during compilation (large native allocations, <code>#load</=
code> in the toplevel).</li>
<li><b>Float arrays and records:</b> this fixes bugs reported by @vouillon =
(<a href=3D"https://github.com/let-def/ocaml/issues/5">#5</a>, <a href=3D"h=
ttps://github.com/let-def/ocaml/issues/6">#6</a>, <a href=3D"https://github=
.com/let-def/ocaml/issues/7">#7</a>), paving the way toward compatibility w=
ith wasm_of_ocaml.</li>
<li><b>Printing:</b> <code>~steps</code> now bounds long lists and arrays, =
and printing is domain-safe.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Introcaml also received the "Claude treatment" to catch bugs I missed :clow=
n_face:.
</p>
</div>
</div>
